WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:03.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:06.780
SPEAKER: Getting started
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.050
Cloud SQL.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:11.050
Cloud SQL is a fully-managed
database service

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.000
that makes it easy to
set up, maintain, manage,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.290
and administer your relational
database in the cloud.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.750
Cloud SQL offers high
performance, scalability,

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.540
and convenience, providing
a database infrastructure

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.180
for applications
running anywhere.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.340
To get started with
Cloud SQL, first

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.130
go to the Cloud SQL Instances
page in the Google Cloud

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.260
Platform console, and
select your project.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.890
Next, click Create Instance.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.480
Click MySQL, and choose
Second Generation.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.480
Enter My Instance
for the instance ID,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.390
and a password
for the root user.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.420
You can use default values
for the other fields,

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.200
and click Create.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.950
You'll return to
the instances list.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.110
Your new instance is
grayed out while it

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:59.142
initializes and starts up.

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:00.600
We can now connect
to your instance

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.990
using the MySQL client
in the Cloud Shell.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.550
In the Google Cloud
platform console,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.890
click the Cloud Shell icon
in the upper right corner.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.880
At the Cloud Shell prompt,
connect to your Cloud SQL

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.460
instance and enter
your root password.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.190
You should now see
the MySQL prompt.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.770
Now that we're connected,
we can perform operations

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.580
on the database.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.750
Create a SQL database on
your Cloud SQL instance

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.270
by typing create
database guestbook.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.710
Insert sample data into
the guestbook database.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.590
And retrieve the data
by selecting select star

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.210
from entries.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.590
Congratulations.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.010
You've successfully created
a Cloud SQL instance.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

